time restricted enough to be straightforward to analyze. Particular
properties of our syntax are the inclusion of unboxed tuples, borrowed
parameters, and the lack of general lambda expressions. The syntax
distinguishes between expressions \emph{e}, and values \emph{v} that
cannot be evaluated further. Values are either variables or fully
applied constructors \emph{Ck}, taking \emph{k} values as arguments. We
often leave out the superscript \emph{k} when not needed.

syntactically rule out that unboxed tuples may be passed as an argument
to a constructor, causing Unboxed tuples (\emph{v}1\emph{, . . ., vk})
are considered expressions, rather than values. In this way, we

\begin{quote}
them to become ``boxed'' (and allocated). Instead of enforcing this with
a type system {[}Peyton Jones and Launchbury 1991{]}, we use this
syntactic restriction to enforce this property. By doing so, the check
is simpler and allows us to specify the FIP calculus independent of its
static semantics. We often write just \emph{v} or \emph{e} for a
singleton unboxed tuple (\emph{v}), and write an overline to denote an
unboxed tuple (\emph{v}1\emph{, . . ., vk}) as\emph{v}, or an unboxed
tuple of variables (\emph{x}1\emph{, . . ., xk}) as\emph{x}. Since
expres-sions always eventually evaluate to an unboxed tuple, the
let\emph{x} = \emph{e}1 in \emph{e}2 expression binds all components of
the result unboxed tuple \emph{e}1 in\emph{x}.

There are no general lambda expressions. In general, closures need to be
heap allocated if they contain free variables. To keep the FIP rules as
simple as possible, we do not allow \emph{arbitrary} lambda expressions.
Instead, the global Σ environment holds all top-level functions \emph{f}
, which can be mutually recursive and passed as arguments. This makes
our calculus essentially second-order. A top-level
\end{quote}

