\textbf{2.2} \textbf{FIP: Fully In-Place}
\end{quote}

As defined, our functional semantics is very liberal and allows
expressions that generally cause allocation, like \emph{C x y}. Figure 4
specifies the FIP calculus rules that guarantee that the resulting
programs can be evaluated without needing any (de)allocation. The
statement Δ \textbar{} Γ ⊢\emph{e} means that under a borrowed
environment Δ and owned environment Γ, the expression \emph{e} is a
well-formed FIP expression. The borrowed environment Δ is a \emph{set}
of borrowed variables which generally come from the borrowed parameters
of a function \emph{f} , or by borrowing in the let rule. We write
Δ\emph{,} Δ′for the union of the sets Δ and Δ′. The owned environment Γ
is a \emph{multiset} of owned variables, and also of size \emph{k}. We
can append two owned environments Γ and Γ′as Γ\emph{,} Γ′. Sometimes, we
also write \emph{reuse credits}. Following Hofmann {[}2000a{]} we
denoted these as a diamond ⋄\emph{k}, signifying a creditΔ\emph{,} Γ to
join a borrowed set with a multi-set Γ where it is required that there
are no reuse credits inΓ, where the result is the borrowed set Δ joined
with the elements in Γ. Note that in the current rules, all variables in
the Γ environment occur only once as we have no way to duplicate them.
In Section 5 we generalize this to the full Perceus calculus.

\begin{quote}
The FIP rules ensure that variables in the owned environment Γ are used
linearly (with some borrowing allowed in let). However, this is a
syntactic property and we do not use a linear \emph{type} system. This
is much simpler to specify and implement, and also makes FIP independent
of any particular type system used by a host language.
\end{quote}

\emph{x} if it is the only element of the owned environment Γ.
Similarly, the tuple rule splits the owned The linearity of the FIP
calculus is apparent in the var rule, Δ \textbar{} \emph{x} ⊢\emph{x}
where we can only \emph{consume}

