The only way to create a fresh constructor with \emph{k} ⩾1 arguments,
is through the reuse rule where we need to check well-formedness of each
argument, but we also need a reuse credit ⋄\emph{k} to guarantee that
the needed space is available at evaluation time. The dmatch! rule
creates such reuse In each branch the \emph{x} is no longer owned though
(but became a reuse credit instead). For simplicity credits: we can
destructively match on an owned variable \emph{x} to get a reuse credit
⋄\emph{k} in each branch. we only allow matching on a variable in the
rules, but we can always rewrite a user expression match! \emph{e} \{
\emph{. . .} \} into let \emph{x} = \emph{e} in match! \emph{x} \{
\emph{..} \} where \emph{x} is a fresh owned variable. Again, we do not
allow freeing at this point, so reuse credits can only be consumed by
the reuse rule or the empty rule for zero-sized reuse credits (when an
atom is matched).

In contrast, the borrowed match bmatch can only match on borrowed
variables and such match can only be used to inspect values without
creating fresh reuse credits. Even though variables in the owned
environment Γ cannot be discarded (i.e. freed) or duplicated (i.e.
shared), we can temporarily \emph{borrow} them. In the let rule the
owned environment is split in three parts Γ1\emph{,} Γ2\emph{,} Γ3. The
Γ1 and Γ3 environments are passed to \emph{e}1 and \emph{e}2
respectively, but the Γ2 environment is passed to \emph{e}2 as an owned
enviroment, but also to \emph{e}1 as a borrowed environment! Since we
\emph{consume} Γ2 in the derivation of \emph{e}2, we can consider them
borrowed in the derivation of \emph{e}1. Note that we still need Γ3
since Γ2 is joined with the borrowed Δ environment and as such cannot
contain any reuse credits (which can thus be included in Γ3 instead).

the owned argument \emph{e}. To allow for passing functions, we can also
