\multicolumn{8}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 14\tabcolsep) * \real{1.0000} + 14\tabcolsep}@{}}{%
\begin{minipage}[t]{\linewidth}\raggedright
With the FIP calculus defined, we can now define another operational
semantics. Figure 5 defines the \emph{store semantics} where we evaluate
using a fixed-size store S. The rules in the store semantics all adhere
to an important invariant: each step of the evaluation should not
allocate or deallocate memory. In this section, we establish a key
result relating this store semantics with the functional operational
semantics defined previously: under certain conditions, satisfied by all
well-formed FIP programs, the store semantics and operational semantics
coincide.

\begin{quote}
The store semantics uses an evaluation context \emph{E} but this time a
full evaluation goes to an unboxed tuple of variables\emph{x} (instead
of values\emph{v}). Any constructor is bound in the store S where every
element is either a binding \emph{x} ↦→1\emph{Ckx}1 \emph{. . . xk} of
size \emph{k}, or a reuse credit ⋄\emph{k} of size \emph{k}. Using the
eval rule, we can reduce using small steps in the store semantics. The
reduction rules have the form S \textbar{} \emph{e} −→s S′\textbar{}
\emph{e}′where an \emph{e} in a store S reduces to \emph{e}′with a new
store S′. The rules are similar to the earlier operational semantics but
now we always substitute with variables instead of values. There are now
two more rules for evaluating constructor values which are bound in the
store. The (\emph{reuses}) transition uses a reuse credit ⋄\emph{k} in
the store to apply the constructor, while
\end{quote}

the latter replaces the original constructor binding with a reuse credit
of the same size. (\emph{atoms}) allows atoms to be created freely. The
(\emph{bmatchs}) and (\emph{dmatchs}) reductions differ, where

\begin{quote}
Since our store semantics is destructive (in the reuse and dmatch
rules), it can fail for expressions where the standard evaluation
semantics would succeed. Even for expressions that are well-formed, the
store semantics can fail if the initial store has internal sharing. If a
shared variable is mutated in place, this would break referential
transparency. Thus, we have to require that any variable is
\end{quote}
\end{minipage}} \\
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

11

\begin{quote}
FP2: Fully in-Place Functional Programming

referred to just once in the store -- we call this a \emph{linear}
store.

\textbf{Definition 1.} (\emph{Store Soundness and Linearity})\\
For a store S we write dom(S) to denote the set of variables \emph{x}
bound in S and write rng(S) to denote the set of values \emph{C x} bound
in S. A store is \emph{sound} if all free variables in rng(S) are bound:
fv(rng(S)) ⊆dom(S). A store is \emph{linear} if it is sound, and any
variable \emph{x} in dom(S) occurs at most once in the free variables of
rng(S). By roots(S) we denote all reuse credits of S and the set of
variables in dom(S) that do not occur in the free variables of rng(S).
\end{quote}

On linear stores mutation is safe; in a reference counted setting such a
store corresponds to a heap where all values have a reference count of
one. We can now state the main soundness theorem. We

We assume that we are given stores corresponding to the owned and
borrowed values, but only write {[}S{]}\emph{x} to denote a substitution
that recursively replaces variables by their bound value in S.

\begin{quote}
require that the store of the owned values is linear. We can then show
that the store evaluation leaves the borrowed values unchanged and only
modifies the owned values:

\textbf{Theorem 1.} (\emph{The store semantics is sound for well-formed
FIP programs})\\
If Δ \textbar{} Γ ⊢\emph{e} and given disjoint stores \emph{S}1\emph{,
S}2 with Δ ⊆dom(\emph{S}1), \emph{S}1 sound, Γ = roots(\emph{S}2) and
\emph{S}2 linear, then {[}\emph{S}1\emph{, S}2{]}\emph{e} ↦−→\emph{S}3
is linear. ∗\emph{v} implies \emph{S}1\emph{, S}2 \textbar{} \emph{e}
↦−→∗s\emph{S}1\emph{, S}3\textbar{} \emph{x} where
{[}\emph{S}3{]}\emph{x} = \emph{v}, \emph{x} = roots(\emph{S}3) and

This is a strong theorem and the proof is quite involved (see Appendix
B), both due to destructive update and the ability to match on variables
temporarily borrowed in the let rule. As a corollary, we can now see
that any FIP expression can run on the store semantics if we use a store
containing the necessary reuse credits, i.e. we give it enough space to
allocate upfront:

\textbf{Corollary 1.}

If \emph{e} ↦−→∗\emph{v} and ∅\textbar{} ⋄\emph{k} ⊢\emph{e},
then⋄\emph{k} \textbar{} \emph{e} ↦−→∗sS \textbar{}\emph{x} and
{[}S{]}\emph{x} =\emph{v}.

Furthermore, any FIP function can be called on stores from the
environment:

\textbf{Corollary 2.}

If Σ\emph{, f} is fully-in-place and \emph{f} (\emph{v}1;\emph{v}2)
↦−→and {[}\emph{S}1{]}\emph{y} =\emph{v}1, {[}\emph{S}2{]}\emph{x}
=\emph{v}2, then also \emph{S}1\emph{, S}2 \textbar{} \emph{f}
(\emph{y};\emph{x}) ↦−→ ∗\emph{v}, and given disjoint linear stores
\emph{S}1\emph{, S}2 with roots \emph{y, x} ∗s\emph{S}1\emph{,
S}3\textbar{}\emph{z}, where {[}\emph{S}3{]}\emph{z} =\emph{v}. We can
define the \emph{size} of a store by adding the sizes of all bindings
within it. Since atoms and empty reuse credits have size zero, they do
not contribute to the size of the store.

\textbf{Definition 2.}
\end{quote}

The size \textbar S\textbar{} of a store S is: \textbar∅\textbar{} = 0
\textbar S\emph{,}⋄\emph{k}\textbar{} = \textbar S\textbar{} + \emph{k}
\textbar S\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{Ckx}1 \emph{. . . xk}\textbar{} =
\textbar S\textbar{} + \emph{k}. With this definition, we can
immediately see that the size of the store doesn't change in any
reduction of the store semantics. As such, FIP programs can reduce
\emph{in-place} without any (de)allocation:

\textbf{Theorem 2.} (\emph{A FIP program reduces in-place.})\\
For any S \textbar{} \emph{e} ↦−→∗sS′ \textbar{} \emph{e}′, we have
\textbar S\textbar{} = \textbar S′\textbar.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{2.4} \textbf{FBIP: Allowing Deallocation}

Our basic FIP calculus is quite strict and allows neither allocation nor
deallocation. We can easily extend it though to allow deallocation.
Figure 6 describes the FBIP calculus as an extension of the FIP calculus
with deallocation, where the syntax is extended with drop \emph{x};
\emph{e} to drop an owned variable \emph{x}, and free \emph{k}; \emph{e}
to free a reuse credit of size \emph{k}.

The drop rule consumes a variable \emph{x} from the owned environment.
Since the multiplicity of all elements in Γ is still one, this asserts
that \emph{x} is no longer an element of Γ. Similarly, the free rule
allows discarding a reuse credit.
\end{quote}

