\begin{quote}
Fig. 7. The FIP or FBIP calculus augmented with bounded allocation

The operational semantics is also extended with two new reductions for
dropping a bound contructor and freeing a reuse credit. With these new
rules and reductions for deallocation, the soundness theorem 1 continues
to hold (see Appendix B). Again, we can immediately see that the store
semantics now only allows deallocation:

\textbf{Theorem 3.} (\emph{A FBIP program can only deallocate.})\\
For any S \textbar{} \emph{e} ↦−→∗sS′ \textbar{} \emph{e}′ with the
deallocation rules, we have \textbar S\textbar{} ⩾\textbar S′\textbar.

In our implementation the fbip keyword checks if the function is
well-formed in the FBIP calculus.

\textbf{2.5} \textbf{FIP and FBIP with bounded allocation}
\end{quote}

We can also model bounded allocation in both the FIP and FBIP calculi to
support the fip(n) and fbip(n) keywords, by adding special
\emph{allocation} credits ⋄to the Γ enviroment. These credits do not
have a size, and can be used to allocate a cell of any size. Since we
usually have several at a time, we write⋄\emph{n}for \emph{n} such
credits.

\begin{quote}
not have to spend allocation credits, which gives them a weakening rule.
We modify the call In the inst rule, we can allocate a reuse credit
⋄\emph{k} of any size from an allocation credit. We do

and deffun rules from above to respect the fip(n) keyword: When calling
a function, we require the necessary allocation credits to call \emph{f}
. Furthermore, we also require allocation credits for all higher-order
functions passed to \emph{f} . Then we can assume in the bapp rule that
any allocation credits necessary for the function call have already been
paid. Finally, in the deffun rule, we obtain as many allocation credits
as assigned by the keyword.

It is slightly tricky to infer the necessary allocation credits when
also inferring deallocation. For example, there can be several, equally
valid inferences for expressions that differ only in their use of
free:\\
• \emph{x} \textbar{} ⋄⊢match \emph{x} \{ \emph{True}
↦→\emph{Cons}(\emph{Nil, Nil}); \emph{False} ↦→\emph{Nil} \} or

The first expression requires an allocation credit, while the second one
requires a reuse credit• \emph{x} \textbar{} ⋄2 ⊢match \emph{x} \{
\emph{True} ↦→\emph{Cons}(\emph{Nil, Nil}); \emph{False} ↦→free 2;
\emph{Nil} \}.

(which is explicitly deallocated). Which one is preferable depends on
