\begin{quote}
So far, our FIP calculus has allowed us to bound the heap space of the
program---but what about the stack space? If we only seek to bound
allocations, we could choose to leave it unbounded. In practice,
however, the stack space matters: when compiling FIP programs to C we
have to assume a relatively small stack and even in a garbage-collected
setting growing the stack is not free. To ensure that the stack is
bounded, we require two modifications to the calculus. We assume that
\end{quote}

any function \emph{f} is defined as part a of mutually recursive
group\emph{f}(which might consist of just \emph{f} or more functions).
In the call rule we then require two additional conditions. Firstly, Σ
contains only functions defined before or as part of the current
mutually recursive group. Secondly, we

constrain the mutually recursive groups\emph{f}by requiring that any
recursive calls within this group are tail-recursive. Formally, all
function definitions \emph{f} ∈\emph{f}need to be of the form \emph{f}
(\emph{y};\emph{x}\uline{)} = \uline{T {[}}\emph{f} \uline{{]}}: T
{[}\emph{f} {]} : := \emph{e}0 \textbar{} \emph{fi}\uline{(}\emph{e}0;
\emph{e}0) \textbar{} let\emph{x} = \emph{e}0 in T {[}\emph{f} {]}
\textbar{} match \emph{e}0 \{ \emph{pi} ↦→T \emph{i}{[}\emph{f} {]} \}
\textbar{} match! \emph{e}0\{ \emph{pi}↦→T \emph{i}{[}\emph{f} {]} \}
\textbar{} drop\emph{\uline{x}}; T {[}\emph{f} {]} \textbar{} free
\emph{k}; T {[}\emph{f} {]}\\
first constraint, these functions can only be defined before or mutually
recursive with the current wher \emph{f} ̸∩fv(\emph{e}0). In the call
rule, one can pass functions from Σ to the called function. By the
definition. In the tail-context we further require that any functions
passed as arguments are also not in the mutually recursive group. Thus,
we can only pass functions that were defined strictly before the current
definition. We call the FIP calculus extended with these requirements
FIPS, in which the stack usage is always bounded. The fip keyword in our
implementation checks if a function is a well-formed FIPSfunction.

\begin{quote}
In a first-order calculus the previous restrictions immediately imply
that the stack size is bounded by the number of mutually recursive
groups: any call is either tail-recursive (and executing it does not
increase the stack size) or to a previously defined group. However, our
FIP calculus is not a first-order calculus since we can pass top-level
functions as arguments. Instead, our FIP calculus is a second-order
calculus: since we ensure that functions never enter the Γ enviroment,
we cannot return any anonymous function as a result.

In a second-order calculus, it is possible for any top-level function to
call back in the list of definitions by calling an anonymous function.
However, any sequence of anonymous calls will be to increasingly earlier
defined functions. Our stack bound is thus equal to the square of the
number of mutually recursive groups.
\end{quote}

To show this formally, we use the size of the evaluation context as a
proxy for the stack size. We write \textbar{}\emph{e}\textbar{} for the
depth of an expression \emph{e} and \textbar{}\emph{E}\textbar{} for the
depth of an evaluation context. We fix a signature Σ and denote by
\textbar{}\emph{𝑒}max\textbar{} the maximum depth of an expression bound
in Σ. Then we have: \textbf{Theorem 4.} (\emph{A FIP program uses
constant stack space})\\
Let Σ be fully-in-place such that for all functions \emph{f} in Σ that
are mutually recursive with\emph{f}, we have \emph{f}
(\emph{y};\emph{x}) = T {[}\emph{f} {]}. At any intermediate evaluation
step S \textbar{} \emph{f} (\emph{y};\emph{x}) ↦−→∗sS′ \textbar{}
\emph{E}{[}\emph{e}′{]}, we have \textbar{}\emph{E}\textbar{}
⩽\textbar{}\emph{𝑒}max\textbar{} · \textbar Σ\textbar2.

This then yields our stack size bound of \textbar Σ\textbar2. The
additional factor of \textbar{}\emph{𝑒}max\textbar{} describes the
maximum size of the evaluation context within each function. In
practice, we would not allocate a stack frame for these parts of the
evaluation context. In a first-order context we would expect a stack
bound of \textbar Σ\textbar{} (where any function can call functions
defined before it). However, in a second-order calculus,\strut
