17

FP2: Fully in-Place Functional Programming

The \$map\emph{𝜏}macro dispatches on the \emph{type} of its argument. if
\emph{x} has type \emph{T 𝛼}it generates a recursive call
\emph{map}(\emph{f} ; \emph{x}) if \emph{x} has type\emph{𝛼}, it
generates a call \emph{f} (\emph{x}); otherwise it leaves the argument
unchanged. In this definition, all recursive calls to \emph{map} happen
in \$\emph{map}, each application of which is in an evaluation context
\emph{Ei} where the side-condition holds. Thus, we can apply the TRMReC
transformation of the previous section and automatically obtain a
tail-recursive fully in-place version of \emph{map}.

Why does reuse work so naturally here? Part of the solution seems to be
that the link to the parent is stored together with the other free
variables. In contrast, McBride {[}2008{]} defines a generic \emph{fold}
function which is not fully in-place since it stores the
defunctionalized continuations on a stack. Nevertheless, as McBride
{[}2001{]} shows, the defunctionalized continuations correspond to the
(generalised) \emph{derivative} of a regular type \emph{T 𝛼}. For every
constructor \emph{C} with \emph{k} recursive subtrees, the derivative
datatype has \emph{k} constructors, one for each possible continuation.
Reuse then arises naturally, as the constructors of the derivative and
original datatype can line up perfectly. In the literature on imperative
algorithms, these traversals that use no extra stack space ex-cept for
direction hints (as encoded in the constructors of the zipper datatype),
are known as Schorr-Waite traversals {[}Schorr and Waite 1967{]}.
Effectively, we can thus derive a Schorr-Waite traversal for any
polynomial algebraic datatype and use the reuse analysis of the FIP
calculus to compile it to the corresponding imperative code. This is
remarkable as imperative Schorr-Waite traversals are notoriously
difficult to get right or prove correct. In his famous work on
separation logic, Reynolds {[}2002{]} writes:

The most ambitious application of separation logic has been Yang's proof
of the Schorr-Waite algorithm for marking structures that contain
sharing and cycles.

Of course, our construction cannot handle cycles, but rather shows the
traversal of trees (or any polynomial inductive datatype in general).
The tree traversal by itself, however, is already quite complicated and
has become a benchmark for verification frameworks {[}Loginov et al.
2006; Walker and Morrisett 2000{]}. Furthermore, our translation also
shows that the Schorr-Waite tree traversal is equivalent to a
stack-based depth-first traversal (like the standard tmap). This was
already shown by Yang {[}2007{]} in the context of separation logic, but
that required more advanced methods than straightforward induction.

\textbf{4} \textbf{FURTHER EXAMPLES OF FULLY IN-PLACE ALGORITHMS}

Many common functions used in functional programming are already FIP in
their standard definition, like map or reverse. In this section we want
to present some advanced examples that test the limits of what is
possible. Along the way, we see several techniques that may be of
general use for designing algorithms in a language with in-place reuse.
These include passing reuse credits to functions, padding constructors
and the partition datastructure. Full listings of the examples in this
section can be found in Appendix A.

\textbf{4.1} \textbf{Imperative red-black tree insertion, functionally}
\end{quote}

Can insertion into a red-black tree be FIP? The traditional
implementation of red-black trees, due to Okasaki {[}1999{]}, can indeed
be written to use all its arguments in-place. However, it occasionally
has to rebalance the result of the recursive call and thus uses stack
space linear in the depth of the tree. However, we can avoid this by
using a zipper (as in Section 3) and balancing while reconstructing the
tree from the zipper. Surprisingly, this yields a functional
implementation which is almost identical to the imperative red-black
tree insertion algorithm described in the popular``Introduction to
Algorithms'' textbook {[}Cormen et al. 2022{]}. We first define the tree
and its zipper:

18

Anton Lorenzen, Daan Leijen, and Wouter Swierstra

\begin{quote}
type color \{ Red; Black \}\\
type tree\textless k,v\textgreater{}\\
Node(c : color, l : tree\textless k,v\textgreater, k : k, v : v, r :
tree\textless k,v\textgreater)\\
Leaf\\
type accum\textless k,v\textgreater{}\\
Done\\
NodeL(c : color, l : accum\textless k,v\textgreater, k : k, v : v, r :
tree\textless k,v\textgreater)\\
NodeR(c : color, l : tree\textless k,v\textgreater, k : k, v : v, r :
accum\textless k,v\textgreater)\\
This definition is typical for a functional datastructure, but different
from the imperative definition as a node does not store a pointer to its
parent. Such parent-pointers would not work well in our setting, because
they create many small cycles between parent and children, which can
never be in-place reused again. Instead, we use pointer-reversal along
the insertion path. On this path, the zipper contains the parent links
\emph{instead of} children links and no cycles are created.

We can insert a value into the tree by recursing into the left- or
right-subtree until we either find the key with an existing value or a
leaf. In the latter case, we will have to allocate a new node for key
and value. During the recursion, we turn the nodes of the tree into the
accum zipper. At the end, we thus have a subtree with our new value and
a zipper. We create a rebalanced red-black tree by calling the fixup
function below on them.

But how do we write fixup? Thankfully, we can translate it almost
verbatim from the rb-insert-fixup procedure in section 13.3 of Cormen et
al. {[}2022{]}! We present the code here to illustrate that this
function is FIP and closely follows its imperative counterpart. A
detailed explanation can be found in {[}Cormen et al. 2022{]}. The
function distinguishes three cases (marked on the left of the function
definition) that correspond to the three cases in the textbook
implementation. Case one translates directly (even if we have it twice).
The second case is the most complicated, rotating an inner part left,
before rotating the outer part right. In case three, it is possible to
stop fixup (by calling rebuild defined below) as the parent node was
colored black.
\end{quote}

fip fun fixup( zp : accum\textless k,v\textgreater, z :
tree\textless k,v\textgreater{} ) : tree\textless k,v\textgreater{}\\
match! zp\\
NodeL(Red,zpp,zpk,zpv,zpr) -\textgreater{} match zpp\\
NodeL(Black,zppp,zppk,zppv,y) -\textgreater{}\\
if is-red(y)\\
then
fixup(zppp,Node(Red,Node(Black,z,zpk,zpv,zpr),zppk,zppv,y.set-black)) //
(1) else
rebuild(zppp,right-rotate(Node(Red,Node(Black,z,zpk,zpv,zpr),zppk,zppv,y)))
// (3) NodeR(Red,zpl,zpk,zpv,zpp) -\textgreater{} match zpp\\
NodeL(Black,zppp,zppk,zppv,y) -\textgreater{}\\
if is-red(y)\\
then
fixup(zppp,Node(Red,Node(Black,zpl,zpk,zpv,z),zppk,zppv,y.set-black)) //
(1) else rebuild(zppp,right-rotate( // (2)
Node(Red,left-rotate(Node(Red,zpl,zpk,zpv,z.set-black)),zppk,zppv,y)))\\
// and cases as above with "left" and "right" interchanged\\
\_ -\textgreater{} rebuild(zp, z)\\
We have left some functions unspecified: set-black sets the color of a
node to black. is-red returns a boolean indicating whether the given
node is red. We have to make its argument borrowed so that we apply r2
to it. The left rotation is as usual (and the right rotation its mirror
image).

\begin{quote}
fip fun is-red(\^{}t : tree) : bool\\
match t \{ Node(Red) -\textgreater{} True; \_ -\textgreater{} False \}

fip fun left-rotate(t : tree\textless k,v\textgreater) :
tree\textless k,v\textgreater{}\\
match! t \{ Node(c,l,k,v,Node(c1,l1,k1,v1,r1)) -\textgreater{}
Node(c1,Node(c,l,k,v,l1),k1,v1,r1); t' -\textgreater{} t' \} The last
remaining function is rebuild, which is called when balancing is
finished. In the imperative implementation, this simply marks the root
black and returns the root. But in our version, the root is now hidden
in the zipper and we have to rebuild the tree from the zipper (without
balancing) to access the root:
\end{quote}

19

\begin{quote}
FP2: Fully in-Place Functional Programming

fip fun rebuild(z : accum\textless k,v\textgreater, t :
tree\textless k,v\textgreater)\\
match! z\\
NodeR(c, l, k, v, z1) -\textgreater{} rebuild(z1, Node(c, l, k, v, t))\\
NodeL(c, z1, k, v, r) -\textgreater{} rebuild(z1, Node(c, t, k, v, r))\\
Done -\textgreater{} t.set-black\\
In practice, the imperative version benefits from not having to rebuild
the tree (and fixup considers some cases specifically to be able to
enter rebuild earlier). Thus we can not quite achieve the same
efficiency in a functional version. However, our version can also be
used persistently (see Section 5) and might be easier to understand.

\textbf{4.2} \textbf{Sorting lists in-place}\\
Is it possible to run merge sort in-place? The traditional functional
implementation first turns each element into a singleton list. A
singleton list is obviously sorted, so we can now pairwise merge such
sorted lists. Finally, we end up with just one sorted list, which we
extract:

{[}4,3,2,1{]} -\textgreater{} {[}{[}4{]},{[}3{]},{[}2{]},{[}1{]}{]} //
create sorted singleton lists\\
-\textgreater{} {[}{[}3,4{]},{[}1,2{]}{]} -\textgreater{}
{[}{[}1,2,3,4{]}{]} // merge pairs of sorted lists\\
-\textgreater{} {[}1,2,3,4{]} // extract sorted list\\
Here, the output takes up just as much space as the input - so a fully
in-place implementation might be possible. But in the first step, many
singleton lists are created for which no reuse tokens are available, so
the traditional implementation is \emph{not} fully in-place.

However, we can make it FIP by using a tailor made datastructure, that
can store the sorted sublists while taking up exactly the same amount of
space as the original list. Our partition is a list partitioned into
sublists, which are either Ones or Subs of at least two elements. We
exploit that we have at least two elements by storing two elements in
the last cell of a list2.

type partition\textless a\textgreater{}\\
Sub(list : list2\textless a\textgreater, tail :
partition\textless a\textgreater)\\
One(elem : a, tail : partition\textless a\textgreater)\\
End\\
type list2\textless a\textgreater{}\\
Cons2(x : a, tail : list2\textless a\textgreater)\\
Nil2(x : a, y : a)\\
Considering the memory usage, we see that a list2 can store \emph{n}
elements in \emph{n} −1 cells. As a result, a partition of \emph{n}
elements uses just as much space as a list of \emph{n} elements, while
also keeping information about the partitioning. With that in hand, we
can implement an in-place list mergesort:

Cons(4,Cons(3,Cons(2,Cons(1,Nil))))\\
-\textgreater{} One(4,One(3,One(2,One(1,End))))\\
-\textgreater{} Sub(Nil2(3,4),Sub(Nil2(1,2),End))-\textgreater{}
Sub(Cons2(1,Cons2(2,Nil2(3,4))),End)-\textgreater{}
Cons(1,Cons(2,Cons(3,Cons(4,Nil))))

// start with unsorted list\\
// created sorted singleton lists // merge pairs of sorted lists\\
// ... until only one list2 is left // convert back to list

Note that this does not solve the (much harder) in-place sorting problem
for arrays, where we can not distinguish sublists through tags and
shuffling pointers.

This datastructure is also helpful to implement a quicksort that can not
run out of stack. The typical functional but in-place implementation is
{[}Baker 1994a; Hofmann 2000b; Hudak 1986{]}:
\end{quote}

fbip fun quicksort(xs : list\textless a\textgreater)\\
match! xs\\
Nil -\textgreater{} Nil\\
Cons(pivot, xx) -\textgreater{}\\
val (lo, hi) = split(pivot, xx) // split xx into (lo,hi) in-place val
(lo',hi') = (quicksort(lo),quicksort(hi))\\
// sort the sublists append(lo', Cons(pivot, hi'))\\
This code is not FIP because the stack usage is not bounded and might
grow linear with the length of the input list. Of course, we could apply
the defunctionalized CPS-transformation (see Section 3), but our
side-condition fails:

20

Anton Lorenzen, Daan Leijen, and Wouter Swierstra

\begin{quote}
Cons(pivot, xx) -\textgreater{} // reuse credit of size 2 available

val (lo, hi) = split(pivot, xx)

quicksort'(lo, Z1(pivot, hi, zipper)) // reuse credit of size 3 needed

The problem here is that the zipper needs to store pivot,hi and the
parent zipper, which requires more space than we have available. This is
because, in a stack-safe quicksort, the zipper needs to keep track of
all the pivots and hi lists that still need to be sorted. However, we
can use a partition structure as the zipper where we store the pivots as
singletons and the hi either not at all (if hi is empty), as a singleton
(if hi is a one-element list) or else as a list2. We can pass the parent
zipper into the split function, which now returns a list lo and a
partition hi which includes the zipper.

Then we obtain a fully in-place solution:

Cons(pivot, xx) -\textgreater{} // reuse credit of size 2 available

val (lo, hi) = split(pivot, xx, zipper)

\textbf{4.3}

quicksort'(lo, One(pivot, hi)) // reuse credit of size 2 needed

\textbf{Finger trees}

Finally, as an advanced example, we want to consider finger trees
{[}Claessen 2020; Hinze and Paterson 2006{]}, an efficient functional
implementation of sequences. Yet, at first glance the cons function on
finger trees does not appear to be FIP: only the More constructors can
be reused as the other datatypes do not match up for reuse. We can fix
this, however, by \emph{padding} all constructors with a dummy atom Pad
so that they all have three slots.
\end{quote}

We use Claessen {[}2020{]}'s version of finger trees, since Hinze and
Paterson's version has an extra Four constructor which would require us
to pad \emph{all} constructors to four fields.

\begin{quote}
fun cons(x : a, s : seq\textless a\textgreater) :
seq\textless a\textgreater{}

match! s

Empty -\textgreater{} Unit(x, Pad, Pad)

Unit(y, \_, \_) -\textgreater{} More(One(x, Pad, Pad), Empty, One(y,
Pad, Pad))

More(One(y, \_, \_), q, u) -\textgreater{} More(Two(x, y, Pad), q, u)

More(Two(y, z, \_), q, u) -\textgreater{} More(Three(x, y, z), q, u)

More(Three(y, z, w), q, u) -\textgreater{} More(Two(x, y, Pad),
cons(Pair(z, w, Pad), q), u)
\end{quote}

We have gotten rid of all deallocations since all constructors on the
left of -\textgreater{} can be paired with one to the right. But we
still have allocations in the Empty, Unit and More(Three) cases. Even
worse, the cons can recurse up to \emph{O}(log \emph{n})-times in the
More(Three) case and require a new memory cell each time, so this
function is not fip(n) or fbip(n). However, this case is very unlikely
as the amortized complexity analysis of finger trees shows that cons
only recurses \emph{O}(1)-times on average and thus only uses a constant
amount of memory.

\begin{quote}
Therefore, we pair a finger tree seq\textless a\textgreater{} with a
buffer which contains exactly the memory needed. Our buffer is just a
padded list of size 3, which makes it available for reuse with the rest
of the finger tree.

type buffer \{ BEmpty; BCons(next : buffer, b : pad, c : pad) \}
\end{quote}

We then pass the necessary reuse credits of size 3 to cons, which we
either use to create a new cell in the finger tree or fill up the
buffer. If we recurse into cons, we draw the neces-sary memory back from
the buffer. Then we only need to ensure that we pass enough credits so
that the buffer is never empty. Inserting two elements x,y into an empty
finger tree yields More(One(x, Pad, Pad), Empty, One(y, Pad, Pad)), so
it would seem that we need to pass at least two credits. But that would
mean that we need 6\emph{n} space to represent \emph{n} elements in a
finger tree! We can do better by specializing More(One) as More0 to
represent the two-element list as More0(x, Empty, One(y, Pad, Pad)).
With this modification, it suffices to pass in a single reuse credit per
element for a space overhead of 3\emph{n} space, which is close to the
2\emph{n} factor of singly-linked lists.

\begin{quote}
Our cons function than takes a reuse credit unit3 and becomes:
\end{quote}

21

\begin{quote}
FP2: Fully in-Place Functional Programming
\end{quote}

