fip fun cons(x : a, u3 : unit3, s : seq\textless a\textgreater, b :
buffer) : (seq\textless a\textgreater, buffer) match! s\\
Empty -\textgreater{} (Unit(x, Pad, Pad), b)\\
Unit(y, \_, \_) -\textgreater{} (More0(x, Empty, One(y, Pad, Pad)), b)\\
More0(y, q, u) -\textgreater{} (More(Pair(x, y, Pad), q, u), b)
\end{quote}

More(Pair(y, z, \_), q, u) -\textgreater{} (More(Triple(x, y, z), q, u),
BCons(b, Pad, Pad))\\
More(Triple(y, z, w), q, u) -\textgreater{}\\
match! b\\
BCons(b', \_, \_) -\textgreater{}\\
val (q', b'') = cons(Pair(z, w, Pad), u3, q, b')\\
(More(Pair(x, y, Pad), q', u), b'')\\
This function is now fully in-place. In the More(Two) case we store an
unneeded credit in the buffer. In the More(Three) case we recurse4and
take a reuse credit from the buffer. The buffer has the invariant that,
given \emph{n}1 Triple, \emph{n}2 Three and \emph{n}3 Two constructors
in the finger tree, its size is just \emph{n}1 + 2 ∗\emph{n}2 +
\emph{n}3. Since this invariant is maintained in the cons function, the
buffer is never empty.

\textbf{5} \textbf{REFERENCE COUNTING WITH BORROWING AND UNBOXING}\\
In this section we formalize the connection between the FIP calculus and
Perceus precise reference counting {[}Lorenzen and Leijen 2022;
Reinking, Xie et al. 2021{]}. Our implementation of FIP in Koka uses
this approach where detection of the uniqueness of owned arguments
happens dynamically at run-time. Figure 9 formalizes the
\emph{𝜆}fipcalculus as an extension of the syntax and operational
semantics of the FBIP calculus (in Figure 4 and 6). It has full lambda
expressions \emph{𝜆zx. e} now since arbitrary allocation is allowed.
Here we write the free variables of the lambda expression explicitly as
(the multiset)\emph{z}. This is not needed for the functional
operational semantics but as we see later, we require it for the heap
based operational semantics. Moreover, we have a dup \emph{x}; \emph{e}
and dropru \emph{x}; \emph{e} expressions that let us duplicate owned
variables and explicitly reuse dropped variables. Finally, the alloc
\emph{k}; \emph{e} allows for abitrary allocation of constructors by
creating reuse credits ⋄\emph{k} at runtime. The (\emph{beta})
evaluation rule for lambda expressions is standard, and we can see that
the (\emph{dup}), \uline{(}\emph{dropru}\uline{)}, and (\emph{alloc})
rules have no effect in the functional operational semantics (and are
only used \uline{4}The recursive call is here in tail-position modulo
product-contexts {[}Leijen and Lorenzen 2023{]}, which can be
efficiently compiled to a tail-recursive call. However, we could also
apply the TRMReC transformation to eliminate it (see section Appendix
D).

22

Anton Lorenzen, Daan Leijen, and Wouter Swierstra

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 0\tabcolsep) * \real{1.0000}}@{}}
\toprule()
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\begin{quote}
in the heap semantics).

We rephrase the original Perceus linear resource calculus, called
\emph{𝜆}1{[}Reinking, Xie et al. 2021{]},
\end{quote}

well-formed, and such expression always uses correct reference counting
when evaluated, i.e. it in Figure 9 as a \emph{type system} (instead of
a typed translation). We call any expressions in Δ \textbar{} Γ
⊢\emph{e}

\begin{quote}
never drops a value from the heap that is still needed later, or leaves
garbage in the heap at the end of an evaluation. Moreover, the new type
rules also extend the original rules with borrowing and unboxed tuples,
and give a characterization of reuse based on reuse credits.

Just like the FIP calculus, the rules are still based on linear logic
with a linear owned Γ environment, but unlike a pure linear logic it now
has an escape hatch: through rules like dup we can freely duplicate
``linear'' variables by maintaining reference counts dynamically at
runtime. As we can see, there is a suprisingly close connection between
\emph{𝜆}fipand the FBIP and FIP calculi where each of \emph{𝜆}fipthat
excludes the rules that require dynamic reference counting! one is a
strict subset of the other: FIP ⊂FBIP ⊂\emph{𝜆}fip. As such, the FIP
calculus is exactly the subset The dup rule is the rule that either
allows us to use a borrowed variable (\emph{x} ∈Δ) as owned, or
duplicates an owned variable (\emph{x} ∈Γ). The alloc rule now allows
arbitrary allocation of a constructor by adding a reuse credit ⋄\emph{k}
to the owned environment. With the full lambda expressions, we also have
an app rule to apply an argument to a lambda expression. Here, we split
the owned environment in two parts for each subexpression. We could have
been more elaborate and allow borrowing of Γ2 in the \emph{e}1
derivation just like our earlier let rule in Figure 4. We refrain from
doing that here for simplicity as we can always use let if borrowing is
required.

The lam rule requires that all free variables of the lambda expression
are owned (which are needed to create the initial closure). In the body,
we check with the free variables (from the closure) and the passed in
parameters as all owned. Borrow information is not part of a type, so
only top-level functions can take borrowed arguments (using the call
rule).

The match rule can match on any borrowed or owned variable. However,
each branch must start by dupping the matched constructor fields (as
dup(\emph{xi})). Indeed, since the match is non-destructive each field
is now reachable directly but also via the original \emph{x} (and thus
we need to increment the reference count at runtime). For simplicity,
the match rule can only match variables but we can always rewrite an
expression match \emph{e} \{ \emph{. . .} \} into let \emph{x} =
\emph{e} in match \emph{x} \{ \emph{. . .} \} for a fresh \emph{x} when
required. Since match no longer creates reuse credits, we can now create
them explicitly instead using the ``drop reuse'' dropru rule. This drops
a variable \emph{x}, and immediately allows for a reuse credit ⋄\emph{k}
where \emph{k} is the allocated size of \emph{x}.\\
With the new match and dropru rules we no longer require the destructive
match and corre-sponding rule of the FIP calculus and we can always
replace any destructive match:

match! \emph{x} \{ \emph{Cixi} →\emph{ei} \} with match \emph{x} \{
\emph{Cixi} →dup\emph{xi}; dropru \emph{x}; \emph{ei} \} . In
particular, if the FIP match! expression is well-formed, we have:

Δ \textbar{} Γ\emph{,xi,}⋄\emph{k} ⊢\emph{ei} (1) dmatch!

Δ \textbar{} Γ\emph{, x} ⊢match! \emph{x} \{ \emph{Cixi} →\emph{ei} \}\\
and thus we can also derive that the translated match is well-formed in
the \emph{𝜆}fipcalculus: Δ \textbar{} Γ\emph{,xi,}⋄\emph{k} ⊢\emph{ei}
(1) dropru\\
match \emph{x} ∈Γ\emph{, x} Δ \textbar{} Γ\emph{, x, xi} ⊢dropru
\emph{x}; \emph{ei}
\end{quote}

Furthermore, unlike the FIP or FBIP calculus, we can always elaborate a
plain expression withΔ \textbar{} Γ\emph{, x} ⊢match \emph{x} \{
\emph{Ci xi} →dup \emph{xi}; dropru \emph{x}; \emph{ei} \}

\begin{quote}
dup, drop, free, alloc, and dropru to make it a well-formed
\emph{𝜆}fipexpression. The heap semantics can thus always be used to
evaluate an expression. In particular, we can easily adapt the Perceus
algorithm {[}Reinking, Xie et al. 2021{]} to elaborate plain expressions
with correct reference count
\end{quote}\strut
