instructions.

\textbf{5.1} \textbf{Elaborating borrowing}

However, the elaboration needs to take some care when functions with
borrowed parameters are passed as arguments. For example, what should
happen when the user passes is-red to map:

map(is-red, {[}t1, t2, t3{]}) : list\textless bool\textgreater{}

fip fun is-red(\^{}t : tree) : bool

fip fun map( l : list\textless a\textgreater, \^{}f : a -\textgreater{}
b ) : list\textless b\textgreater{}

Here, map assumes that the given function f takes its argument as owned,
while is-red takes its argument t as borrowed. In a linear type system
(and the FIP calculus), such a program would be rejected. However, we
follow a different approach here: Since we seek to integrate fully
in-place programs seamlessly into a general-purpose functional
programming language, the program above should be accepted. Instead, we
\emph{wrap} functions with borrowed parameters, into a closure that
takes only owned parameters, calls the original function and then drops
as necessary {[}Ullrich and de Moura 2019{]}. Formally, we can derive:
\end{quote}

