\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\emph{y} \textbar{}\emph{x} ⊢\emph{x} call ∅\textbar{} \emph{z}
⊢\emph{z} drop\\
\emph{y} \textbar{}\emph{\uline{x}} ⊢\emph{f}
\uline{(}\emph{y};\emph{x}) ∅\uline{\textbar{}} \emph{y, z}
⊢drop\emph{y}; \emph{z} let\\
∅\textbar{}\emph{y,\uline{x}} ⊢let \emph{z} = \emph{f}
(\emph{y};\uline{\emph{x})} in drop\emph{y}; \emph{z} lam\\
Δ \textbar{} ∅⊢\emph{𝜆}∅(\emph{y,x})\emph{.} let \emph{z} = \emph{f}
(\emph{\uline{y}};\emph{x}) in drop \emph{y}; \emph{z}\\
This wrapping can be done cheaply for top-level functions with borrowed
parameters, since the new closure has no free variables and can be
lambda-lifted to the top-level without allocation. Later compilation
stages may then decide to inline the nested function call -- making the
wrapping zero-overhead at the cost of code duplication.

However, this would be more expensive on closures, where \emph{f} itself
is a free variable! Then we would have to allocate the wrapping closure
and could not rely on inlining to remove the overhead of the extra
indirection. For this reason, borrowing is usually not done on closures
-- and we reflect this in our FIP and \emph{𝜆}fipcalculi to remain
compatible with this interpretation of reference counting.\strut
\end{minipage}} \\
