\begin{quote}
First we generalize the properties of the store semantics to reference
counted heaps:\\
\textbf{Definition 3.} (\emph{Heap Soundness and Linearity})\\
For a heap H we write dom(H) to denote the set of variables \emph{x}
bound in H and write rng(H) to denote the set of values \emph{C x} bound
in H. Two heaps \emph{H}1\emph{, H}2 are compatible if they map equal
names \emph{x} ↦→\emph{nv} ∈\emph{H}1\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{mw} ∈\emph{H}2,
to equal values \emph{v} = \emph{w}. A heap is \emph{sound} if all free
variables in 25

FP2: Fully in-Place Functional Programming

rng(H) are bound: fv(rng(H)) ⊆dom(H). A heap is \emph{linear} if it is
sound, and any variable \emph{x} ↦→\emph{nv} in dom(H) occurs at most
\emph{n} times in the free variables of rng(H). By roots(H) we denote
the multi-set of reuse credits of H and variables \emph{x} ↦→\emph{nv}
of dom(H), which contains any variable \emph{n} −\emph{m} times, if it
occurs \emph{m} times in the free variables of rng(H).
\end{quote}

The definition of linearity ensures that mutation is safe if the
reference count is one. Exactly as in the store semantics, we write
{[}H{]}\emph{x} to denote a substitution that recursively replaces
variables by their bound value in H. We assume that we are given heaps
corresponding to the owned and borrowed values, but only require that
the heap of the owned values is linear and do not assume that the heaps
have a disjoint domain. Instead we use the \emph{join} operator ⊗to
define a joined heap of the borrowed and owned part, even if they have
common elements with the same name and value. We join common elements by
summing their reference counts. Since this eliminates one reference to
their children, we decrease their reference count accordingly:

\begin{quote}
∅⊗\emph{H}2 = \emph{H}2

\emph{H}1\emph{,}⋄\emph{k} ⊗\emph{H}2 = \emph{H}1
⊗\emph{H}2\emph{,}⋄\emph{k}\\
\emph{H}1\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{nv} ⊗\emph{H}2 = \emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2\emph{,
x} ↦→\emph{nv} iff \emph{x} ̸∈dom(\emph{H}2)\\
\emph{H}1\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{nv} ⊗\emph{H}2\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{mv, z}
↦→\emph{k}+1\emph{w} = \emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2\emph{, x}
↦→\emph{n}+\emph{mv, z} ↦→\emph{kw} iff\emph{z} = fv(\emph{v})\\
The rootset of \emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2 is exactly the disjoint union of the
roots of \emph{H}1 and \emph{H}2. When applied to linear heaps, ⊗can be
viewed as a partial commutative monoid {[}Jung et al. 2015{]} where
every result is valid. However, we prefer a categorical view on linear
heaps where a morphism \emph{H}1 →\emph{H}2 exists if dom(\emph{H}1)
⊆dom(\emph{H}2) and roots(\emph{H}1) ⊆roots(\emph{H}2). This forms a
monoidal category with the join operator as tensor product. We also
define a heap subtraction operation {[}\emph{H}1\emph{, H}2{]} if
\emph{H}1 →\emph{H}2 similar to an internal hom (and which corresponds
to the magic wand of separation logic). We can then show that heap
evaluation leaves the borrowed values unchanged:

\textbf{Theorem 6.} (\emph{The heap semantics is sound for well-formed
Perceus programs})\\
If Δ \textbar{} Γ ⊢\emph{e} and given heaps \emph{H}1\emph{, H}2 with Δ
⊆dom(\emph{H}1), \emph{H}1 sound, Γ = roots(\emph{H}2) and \emph{H}2
linear, then {[}\emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2{]}\emph{e} ↦−→and \emph{H}3 is
linear. ∗\emph{v} implies \emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2 \textbar{} \emph{e}
↦−→∗h\emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}3 \textbar{} \emph{x} where
{[}\emph{H}3{]}\emph{x} = \emph{v}, \emph{x} = roots(\emph{H}3)

This is again a strong theorem as it shows that the dynamic reference
count is always correct and no variables will be discarded too early,
while also having no garbage at the end of an evaluation (\emph{x} =
roots(\emph{H}3)). Our proof (in Appendix E) is novel and may be well
suited to possible mechanized formalization. As a corollary, any closed
\emph{𝜆}fipexpression can evaluate starting from an empty heap:

\textbf{Corollary 3.}

If \emph{e} ↦−→∗\emph{v} and ∅\textbar{} ∅⊢\emph{e}, then ∅\textbar{}
\emph{e} ↦−→∗hH \textbar{}\emph{x} and {[}H{]}\emph{x} =\emph{v}.
\end{quote}

While it is outside the scope of this paper, we could also modify the
let rule of our calculus with a

However, borrowing makes it harder to achieve these proporties and
