\begin{quote}
FP2: Fully in-Place Functional Programming

rng(\emph{S}1). Since it does not occur in the free variables of
rng(\emph{S}2), it also occurs at most once in the free variables of
rng(\emph{S}1\emph{, S}2).

\textbf{Case} Roots: Since \emph{S}1 is sound and disjoint from
\emph{S}2, we have Γ2 ∩fv(rng(\emph{S}1)) = ∅. By symme-try, Γ1\emph{,}
Γ2 ⊆roots(\emph{S}1\emph{, S}2). Assume \emph{x} ∈roots(\emph{S}1\emph{,
S}2). Then \emph{x} ∈roots(\emph{S}1) or \emph{x} ∈roots(\emph{S}2) and
so \emph{x} ∈Γ1\emph{,} Γ2.

\textbf{B.2 Simple Invariant}

Our simple invariant maintains that the store remains ``well-formed''
during evaluation. We use two stores: a ``borrowed'' store \emph{S}1
which is unchanged by evaluation and an ``owned'' store which can be
changed. Our simple invariant \emph{I} (\emph{e,} Δ\emph{,} Γ\emph{,
S}1\emph{, S}2) is defined as:\\
• Δ \textbar{} Γ ⊢\emph{e}\\
• \emph{S}1\emph{, S}2 are disjoint stores\\
• Δ ⊆dom(\emph{S}1) and \emph{S}1 sound
\end{quote}

That invariant makes it safe to modify \emph{S}2, as all values that the
store semantics destroys are in• Γ = roots(\emph{S}2) and \emph{S}2
linear

\begin{quote}
Γ and not used anywhere else in the store (Γ = roots(\emph{S}2)). It
would be enough for soundness to demand Γ ⊆roots(\emph{S}2). However,
the stronger assertion directly gives us the garbage-free theorem and by
the weakening lemma 4 we can always add separated memory to the store
later:

\textbf{Lemma 4.} (\emph{Weakening for store semantics})\\
If S \textbar{} \emph{e} −→∗sS′ \textbar{} \emph{e}′ then S\emph{,
S}1−→∗sS′\emph{, S}1\textbar{} \emph{e}′ for any \emph{S}1with
dom(\emph{S}1) ∩dom(S) = ∅.

\textbf{Proof}. By straight-forward induction on the judgement S
\textbar{} \emph{e} −→∗sS′ \textbar{} \emph{e}′.

\textbf{B.3 Progress}

In this section we want to show that the store semantics can progress if
the simple invariant is true and operational semantics can progress. We
assume throughout that for any function \emph{f} (\emph{y};\emph{x}) =
\emph{e} ∈Σ, we have fv(\emph{e}) ⊆\emph{y,x}. This is true if Σ is
fully in-place:\\
\textbf{Lemma 5.} (\emph{Free variables of FIP expressions are in}
Δ\emph{,} Γ)\\
