We now want to generalize lemma 13 to arbitrary evaluation contexts. In
particular, we might start with \emph{I} (\emph{E}{[}\emph{e}{]}\emph{,}
Δ\emph{,} Γ\emph{, S}1\emph{, S}2), take a step \emph{S}1\emph{, S}2
\textbar{} \emph{e} −→s \emph{S}1\emph{, S}3 \textbar{} \emph{e}′and
want to obtain \emph{I} (\emph{E}{[}\emph{e}′{]}\emph{,} Δ′\emph{,}
Γ′\emph{, S}1\emph{, S}3). But unfortunately, this does not work. Let's
see where this breaks down. We define:\\
• \emph{E} = let\emph{x} = □in drop\emph{x}; \emph{y}

\begin{quote}
Then:• \emph{e} = match \emph{y} \{ \emph{C y} ↦→\emph{f} (\emph{y}; )
\}

• We start with \emph{I} (\emph{E}{[}\emph{e}{]}\emph{,} ∅\emph{,}
\{\emph{y}\}\emph{,} ∅\emph{, S}2)\\
• We have \emph{y} \textbar{} ∅⊢\emph{e}, so \emph{I} (\emph{e,}
\uline{\{}\emph{y}\}\emph{,} ∅\emph{, S}2\emph{,} ∅)\\
• We step \emph{S}2 \textbar{} match \emph{y} \{ \emph{C \uline{y}}
↦→\emph{f} (\emph{y}; ) \uline{\}} −→s \emph{S}2 \textbar{} \emph{f}
(\emph{y}; )

But then we do not have:• Now we have \emph{y} \textbar{} ∅⊢\emph{f}
(\emph{y}; ), so \emph{I} (\emph{e}′\emph{,} \{\emph{y}\}\emph{,}
∅\emph{, S}2\emph{,} ∅)

• \emph{I} (\emph{E}{[}\emph{f} (\emph{y}; ){]}\emph{,} ∅\emph{,}
\{\emph{y}\}\emph{,} ∅\emph{, S}2), because the let-rule only allows us
to borrow \emph{y}, not\emph{y}• \emph{I} (\emph{E}{[}\emph{f}
(\emph{y}; ){]}\emph{,} ∅\emph{,} \{\emph{y,y}\}\emph{,} ∅\emph{, S}2),
because\emph{y} are not used as owned.
\end{quote}

The problem seems to be that the Δ environment of the nested computation
can change in such• \emph{I} (\emph{E}{[}\emph{f} (\emph{y};
){]}\emph{,} \{\emph{y}\}\emph{,} \{\emph{y}\}\emph{,} ∅\emph{, S}2),
because clearly \emph{y} ̸∈∅.

\begin{quote}
a way, that we can not put the enviroment back together in our
invariant. Therefore, we need a weaker assumption which is maintained
across nested evaluation steps. In this weaker invariant, we maintain
the split of the evaluation context explicitly and allow a new
Δ′environment for the contained expression. We define
\emph{IE}(\emph{e,} Δ\emph{,} Γ\emph{, S}1\emph{, S}2) by induction on
\emph{E}:\\
