We will also need that the tail-context is preserved by evaluation. Here
we use the T {[}\emph{f} {]} tail-context, which includes the condition,
that \emph{fi} ̸∈fv(\emph{e}0). This is important, since it means that a
tail-call can not pass along a function pointer of the same recursive
group. If we didn't have that restriction, functions in the same
recursive group could call themselves anonymously in non-tail position
and the stack size would be unbounded.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Lemma 37.} (\emph{Substitution preserves tail contexts})\\
If \emph{e} = T {[}\emph{f} {]} and\emph{f} ∩\emph{y} = ∅, then
\emph{e}{[}\emph{x}:=\emph{y}{]} = T {[}\emph{f} {]}.
\end{quote}

