\textbf{D} \textbf{TRMC WITH REUSE TRANSLATION}

In this section, we prove the soundness of the TRMC translation with
reused context. We base our

formalization on the slightly generalised presentation in figure 12. In
particular, the constructors of

our zipper are generated by an accumulator function \emph{AccE}, which
the version in the paper would

always call on ⋄\emph{k,zi} with \emph{k} = \textbar{}\emph{zi}\textbar.
But what if \emph{k} ≥\textbar{}\emph{zi}\textbar{} + 1? We can still
represent this by padding the accumulator with atoms \emph{Cunit}.
Similarly, if several reuse credits are available, we create a list of

accumulators that jointly stores all free variables.

\textbf{D.1} \textbf{Soundness}

\textbf{Lemma 41.} (\emph{Accumulators are FIP})

If \emph{AccE}(Γ; \emph{k}) exists, then ∅\textbar{} Γ\emph{, k}
⊢\emph{AccE}(Γ; \emph{k}).

\textbf{Proof}. By induction on \emph{AccE}(Γ; \emph{k}):

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Case} \emph{AccE}(∅; \emph{k}) = \emph{k}: By the var rule we
have ∅\textbar{} \emph{k} ⊢\emph{k}.

\textbf{Case} \emph{AccE}(Γ\emph{,}⋄\emph{k, x}1\emph{, . . ., xn};
\emph{k}) = \emph{Ck Ex}1 \emph{. . . xn AccE}(Γ; \emph{k}) \emph{Cunit
. . . Cunit}:

∅\textbar{} Γ\emph{, k} ⊢\emph{AccE}(Γ; \emph{k})

∅\textbar{} ∅⊢\emph{Cunit}

∅\textbar{} \emph{xi} ⊢\emph{xi}

∅\textbar{} Γ\emph{,}⋄\emph{k, x}1\emph{, . . ., xn, k} ⊢\emph{Ck Ex}1
\emph{. . . xn AccE}(Γ; \emph{k}) \emph{Cunit . . . Cunit}\\
(1), by inductive hypothesis
\end{quote}

