\begin{quote}
\textbf{E} \textbf{SOUNDNESS OF HEAP SEMANTICS}

This section contains the soundness result for the heap semantics. Since
the heap semantics extends the store semantics, we will follow the same
proof structure. In particular, many lemmata hold unchanged (just
replacing −→cases to each lemma. We extend the free variables as
usual:∗sby −→∗h, S with H, using ⊗, etc). Thus we only add the extra

fv(dup\emph{x}; \emph{e}) fv(dropru\emph{x}; \emph{e}) fv(alloc
\emph{k}; \emph{e}) fv(\emph{𝜆zx. e}) := := := := fv(\emph{e})\emph{, x}
fv(\emph{e})\emph{, x} fv(\emph{e}) fv(\emph{e}) −\emph{x}

\textbf{E.1} \textbf{Properties of Heaps}

As before, we will often need to focus on a specific part of the heap
that corresponds to all values reachable from a root set Γ. In this
section, we generalize the properties of stores to heaps using the join
operator:

∅⊗\emph{H}2 = \emph{H}2

\emph{H}1\emph{,}⋄\emph{k} ⊗\emph{H}2 = \emph{H}1
⊗\emph{H}2\emph{,}⋄\emph{k}\\
\emph{H}1\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{nv} ⊗\emph{H}2 = \emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2\emph{,
x} ↦→\emph{nv} iff \emph{x} ̸∈dom(\emph{H}2)\\
\emph{H}1\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{nv} ⊗\emph{H}2\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{mv, z}
↦→\emph{k}+1\emph{w} = \emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2\emph{, x}
↦→\emph{n}+\emph{mv, z} ↦→\emph{kw} iff\emph{z} = fv(\emph{v})\\
This definition still works if \emph{H}1 contains cycles. We can move
the elements of the cycle in any order into \emph{H}2. It might be
attractive to decrease the counts of\emph{z} in \emph{H}1 itself, since
this would correspond to ``deleting'' the element \emph{x} from
\emph{H}1. However, with that modification, we could not handle cycles,
because then the children\emph{z} of the last cycle element would be in
\emph{H}2 instead of \emph{H}1.

We write \#\emph{x}Γ to denote the number of times \emph{x} occurs in Γ,
\#\emph{xH}1 to denote the number of times it occurs free in
rng(\emph{H}1) and \#\emph{x}(\emph{H}1 ∩\emph{H}2) to denote the number
of times it occurs free in rng(\emph{H}1) ∩rng(\emph{H}2).
\end{quote}

\textbf{Lemma 47.} (\emph{Counts in joined heaps})\\
We have \#\emph{x}(\emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2) = \#\emph{xH}1 + \#\emph{xH}2
−\#\emph{x}(\emph{H}1 ∩\emph{H}2).

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Proof}. Since the join operator merges compatible \emph{v}
∈rng(\emph{H}1) ∩rng(\emph{H}2).
\end{quote}

\textbf{Lemma 48.} (\emph{Distributivity of counting})\\
We have \#\emph{x}((\emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2) ∩\emph{H}3) =
\#\emph{x}(\emph{H}1 ∩\emph{H}3) + \#\emph{x}(\emph{H}2 ∩\emph{H}3)
−\#\emph{x}(\emph{H}1 ∩\emph{H}2 ∩\emph{H}3). \textbf{Proof}. By lemma
47 on those \emph{v} also in \emph{H}3.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Lemma 49.} (\emph{Joining heaps})\\
Let \emph{H}1 be a sound/linear heap with roots Γ1 and \emph{H}2 a
linear heap with roots Γ2. Then \emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2 is a sound/linear
heap with roots Γ1\emph{,} Γ2.

\textbf{Proof}.

\textbf{Case} Well-definedness: The reference counts of the\emph{z} can
become zero temporarily. The count can not become negative, because we
only decrease the count of\emph{z} if their parent is also in \emph{H}2.
This can only happen as often as the\emph{z} occur free in
rng(\emph{H}2). Since \emph{H}2 is linear, the count of\emph{z} is at
least the number of times\emph{z} occurs free in rng(\emph{H}2).
Furthermore, since\emph{z} has a reference count of at least one in
\emph{H}1, we will end up with a positive reference count in the end.

\textbf{Case} Soundness: The invariant that \emph{x} ∈dom(\emph{H}1
⊗\emph{H}2) iff \emph{x} ∈dom(\emph{H}1) or \emph{x} ∈dom(\emph{H}2) is
main-tained in all cases of the definition ⊗. Since \emph{H}1 and
\emph{H}2 are sound, then so is \emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2.
\end{quote}

