⊗\emph{H}3)) −\#\emph{x}(\emph{H}2 ∩\emph{H}3) (3), reference count in
\emph{H}1 ⊗(\emph{H}2 ⊗\emph{H}3)

\textbf{Lemma 51.} (\emph{Symmetry of joining heaps})

Let \emph{H}1\emph{, H}2 be linear heaps. Then \emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2 =
\emph{H}2 ⊗\emph{H}1.

\textbf{Proof}. Clearly, dom(\emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2) =
dom(\emph{H}1)\emph{,} dom(\emph{H}2) = dom(\emph{H}2)\emph{,}
dom(\emph{H}1) = dom(\emph{H}2 ⊗\emph{H}1). By lemma 47, the reference
counts are the same.
\end{quote}

We can define a category of linear, compatible heaps, where there is a
unique morphism \emph{f} : \emph{H}1 →\emph{H}2 between \emph{H}1\emph{,
H}2 iff dom(\emph{H}1) ⊆dom(\emph{H}2) and roots(\emph{H}1)
⊆roots(\emph{H}2). In that case we just write

\begin{quote}
\emph{H}1 →\emph{H}2.

\textbf{Lemma 52.} (\emph{Monotocity of joining heaps})

Let \emph{H}1 →\emph{H}2 and \emph{H}3 →\emph{H}4. Then \emph{H}1
⊗\emph{H}3 →\emph{H}2 ⊗\emph{H}4.

\textbf{Proof}. As before, the variables of \emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}3 are
those of \emph{H}1\emph{, H}3. Those variables occur in \emph{H}2\emph{,
H}4 and thus \emph{H}2 ⊗\emph{H}4. By lemma 49, the roots of \emph{H}1
⊗\emph{H}3 is the disjoint union of the roots of \emph{H}1 and
\emph{H}3. By assumption, these are a subset of the roots of \emph{H}2
and \emph{H}4, which give the roots of \emph{H}2 ⊗\emph{H}4.

This shows that the category is monoidal with the join operator as the
tensor product and the

empty heap as tensor unit. For a linear heap with Γ ⊆roots(H), we write
H{[}Γ{]} for the smallest linear subset of H containing Γ. Next we want
to define a heap subtraction operator, which will

become the internal hom:

{[}(\emph{H}1\emph{,}⋄\emph{k})\emph{,} (\emph{H}2\emph{,}⋄\emph{k}){]}
= {[}\emph{H}1\emph{, H}2{]}
\end{quote}

