\textbf{Proof}.

\textbf{Case} Well-definedness: The operator acts in two stages. In the
first stage, all reuse credits are

removed from \emph{H}1. Since roots(\emph{H}1) ⊆roots(\emph{H}2), each
reuse credit in \emph{H}1 has a matching one in \emph{H}2.

Then we recursively remove all \emph{x} ∈dom(\emph{H}1) which do not
occur free in rng(\emph{H}2). Since \emph{H}1 →\emph{H}2, any \emph{x}
∈dom(\emph{H}1) is also in dom(\emph{H}2) with no bigger reference
count. In the second stage, we adjust the reference counts of all other
such \emph{x}.

\textbf{Case} Soundness: \emph{H}2 is sound by assumption and we only
delete \emph{x} from \emph{H}2 if it is not free in rng(\emph{H}2).

on the reference counts. After the first stage, \emph{H}1 and \emph{H}2
are still linear, even if both have new roots. \textbf{Case} Linearity:
Clearly, {[}\emph{H}1\emph{, H}2{]} contains all reuse tokens of
\emph{H}2 not contained in \emph{H}1. Let us focus

In the second stage, the reference count of any \emph{x} ∈dom(\emph{H}2)
becomes:

\emph{n} = \#\emph{x}Γ1 + \#\emph{xH}1 \emph{n} + \emph{m} =
\#\emph{x}Γ2 + \#\emph{xH}2\\
\emph{m} + \#\emph{x}(\emph{H}1 ∩{[}\emph{H}1\emph{, H}2{]})\\
= \#\emph{x}Γ2 + \#\emph{xH}2 −\#\emph{x}Γ1 −\#\emph{xH}1 +
\#\emph{x}(\emph{H}1 ∩{[}\emph{H}1\emph{, H}2{]})\\
= \#\emph{x}Γ2 + \#\emph{xH}2 −\#\emph{x}Γ1 +
\#\emph{x}{[}\emph{H}1\emph{, H}2{]} −\#\emph{x}(\emph{H}1
⊗{[}\emph{H}1\emph{, H}2{]})\\
= \#\emph{x}Γ2 −\#\emph{x}Γ1 + \#\emph{x}{[}\emph{H}1\emph{, H}2{]} (1),
since \emph{H}1 linear
\end{quote}

