Anton Lorenzen, Daan Leijen, and Wouter Swierstra

\begin{quote}
\textbf{E.2} \textbf{Simple Invariant}

Our simple invariant maintains that the heap remains ``well-formed''
during evaluation. We use

two heaps: a ``borrowed'' heap \emph{H}1 which is unchanged by
evaluation and an ``owned'' heap which

can be changed. Our simple invariant \emph{I} (\emph{e,} Δ\emph{,}
Γ\emph{, H}1\emph{, H}2) is defined as:

• Δ \textbar{} Γ ⊢\emph{e}\\
• \emph{H}1\emph{, H}2 are compatible heaps\\
• Δ ⊆dom(\emph{H}1) and \emph{H}1 sound

That invariant makes it safe to modify \emph{H}2, as all values that the
heap semantics destroys are in Γ• Γ = roots(\emph{H}2) and \emph{H}2
linear

and not used anywhere else in the heap (Γ = roots(\emph{H}2)). It would
be enough for soundness to demand Γ ⊆roots(\emph{H}2). However, the
stronger assertion directly gives us the garbage-free theorem and by the
weakening lemma 64 we can always add separated memory to the heap later.

Unlike in the store semantics, the weaking lemma does not hold for every
step. If we have an

expression like drop \emph{x}, this will lead to different steps
depending on whether the reference count is

one or not. Therefore, we need to characterize dropping and dupping in
terms of the final heap:

\textbf{Lemma 61.} (\emph{Dupping joins a root})

If H \textbar{} dup \emph{x}; \emph{e} −→h H′\textbar{} \emph{e} then
H′= H ⊗H{[}\emph{x}{]}.

\textbf{Proof}. We have H\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{nv} \textbar{} dup \emph{x};
\emph{e} −→h H\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{n}+1\emph{v} \textbar{} \emph{e}. Since
dom(H ⊗H{[}\emph{x}{]}) = dom(H) and roots(H ⊗H{[}\emph{x}{]}) =
roots(H)\emph{, x}, we have the claim.

\textbf{Lemma 62.} (\emph{Dropping subtracts a root})

If \emph{x} is a root of H and H \textbar{} drop \emph{x}; \emph{e}
−→∗hH′ \textbar{} \emph{e} then H′ = {[}H{[}\emph{x}{]}\emph{,} H{]}.

\textbf{Proof}. We show the statement for all \emph{x} by induction over
all H with H{[}\emph{x}{]} →H. In the inductive hypothesis, we can
assume the claim for all \emph{y} and H′⊊H with H′{[}\emph{y}{]} →H′.

\textbf{Case} If the reference count of \emph{x} in H is one, then
\emph{H}1\emph{, x} ↦→1\emph{v} \textbar{} drop \emph{x}; \emph{e} −→h
\emph{H}1 \textbar{} drop\emph{z}; \emph{e}. Since\\
\emph{H}1 ⊊H and the\emph{z} are roots of \emph{H}1, we can apply the
inductive hypothesis to obtain H′= {[}\emph{H}1{[}\emph{z}1{]}\emph{, .
. .} {[}\emph{H}1{[}\emph{zn}{]}\emph{, H} Since H{[}\emph{x}{]} =
(\emph{H}1{[}\emph{z}1{]} ⊗\emph{. . .}
⊗\emph{H}1{[}\emph{zn}{]})\emph{, x} ↦→1\emph{v} this implies the claim.
\end{quote}

\textbf{Case} If the reference count of \emph{x} in H is bigger than
one, then \emph{H}1\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{n}+1\emph{v} \textbar{} drop
\emph{x}; \emph{e} −→h \emph{H}1\emph{, x} ↦→\emph{nv} \textbar{}
\emph{e}. Since {[}H{[}\emph{x}{]}\emph{,} H{]} =H{[}\emph{x}{]}\emph{,}
H, every variable except \emph{x} will have an unchanged count and only
the count of \emph{x} will be decreased by one.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Lemma 63.} (\emph{Dropru subtracts a root and adds a credit})

If H \textbar{} dropru \emph{x}; \emph{e} −→∗hH′ \textbar{} \emph{e}
then H′ = {[}H{[}\emph{x}{]}\emph{,} H{]} ⊗⋄\emph{k}.

\textbf{Proof}. The rule for dropru acts like the rule for drop, except
that it puts a space credit into the

heap. The claim thus follows from lemma 62.

Then we can weaken an evaluation if it completes all dropping
procedures: we over-approximate

this by requesting that the final \emph{e}′does not start with a drop.
The interesting part of the proof is

that we can move \emph{H}1 out of the joins and subtractions induced by
dups/drops by lemma 50 and 60.

