However, notice that we introduce a Δ′enviroment at every level of the
evaluation context. That means, that we can only remove a single level
of the evaluation context at a time. To model this, we call an
evaluation context \emph{E} flat if \emph{E} = \emph{E}′{[}\emph{E}′′{]}
implies that either \emph{E}′or \emph{E}′′is the hole. Then:
\textbf{Lemma 79.} (\emph{Strengthening the invariant})\\
If \emph{E} is flat and \emph{IE}(\emph{v,} Δ\emph{,} Γ\emph{,
H}1\emph{, H}2), then \emph{I} (\emph{E}{[}\emph{v}{]}\emph{,} Δ\emph{,}
Γ\emph{, H}1\emph{, H}2).

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Lemma 80.} (\emph{Strengthening the invariant})\\
If \emph{E}2 is flat and \emph{IE}{[}\emph{E}2{]}(\emph{v,} Δ\emph{,}
Γ\emph{, H}1\emph{, H}2), then \emph{IE}(\emph{E}2{[}\emph{v}{]}\emph{,}
Δ\emph{,} Γ\emph{, H}1\emph{, H}2).

\textbf{E.6} \textbf{Soundness}

Now we can show the main soundness theorem. As earlier, we extend the
progress and preservation proofs to handle the step and eval cases. If
we evaluate \emph{e}1 under the context \emph{E}1, we need to assume the
invariant \emph{IE}1(\emph{e}1\emph{,} Δ\emph{,} Γ\emph{, H}1\emph{,
H}2). But how can we obtain the invariant for this precise \emph{E}1?
The trick is that we do not have to know \emph{E}1, instead we can just
assume \emph{IE}2 (\emph{e}2\emph{,} Δ\emph{,} Γ\emph{, H}1\emph{, H}2)
for \emph{E}1{[}\emph{e}1{]} = \emph{E}2{[}\emph{e}2{]}: \textbf{Lemma
81.} (\emph{Comparing evaluation contexts})\\
Let \emph{E}1{[}\emph{e}1{]} = \emph{E}2{[}\emph{e}2{]}, then either:•
\emph{E}1 = \emph{E}′• \emph{E}2 =
\emph{E}′1{[}\emph{E}′′2{[}\emph{E}′′1{]} with \emph{E}′2{]} with
\emph{E}′1= \emph{E}2 and \emph{e}2 = \emph{E}′′2= \emph{E}1 and
\emph{e}1 = \emph{E}′′1{[}\emph{e}1{]} 2{[}\emph{e}2{]}

\textbf{Lemma 82.} (\emph{Alignment of invariant})\\
If \emph{e}1 −→\emph{e}′1, \emph{E}1{[}\emph{e}1{]} =
\emph{E}2{[}\emph{e}2{]} and \emph{IE}2(\emph{e}2\emph{,} Δ\emph{,}
Γ\emph{, H}1\emph{, H}2), then \emph{IE}1(\emph{e}1\emph{,} Δ\emph{,}
Γ\emph{, H}1\emph{, H}2).

\textbf{Lemma 83.} (\emph{Heap semantics can progress})\\
If \emph{IE}(\emph{e,} Δ\emph{,} Γ\emph{, H}1\emph{, H}2) and
{[}\emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H}2{]}\emph{e} −→\emph{e}′, then \emph{H}1
⊗\emph{H}2 \textbar{} \emph{e} −→∗h\emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H} ′2\textbar{}
\emph{e}′′ with \emph{e}′ = {[}\emph{H}1 ⊗\emph{H} ′2{]}\emph{e}′′.

